module tools.multi_module_compile_fixture

import std.types { String, NonEmptyStr, Bool, Int, List }
import gunbc.compile_diagnostic_census { CompileDiagnosticCensusRow }

// THE ISOLATED MULTI-MODULE COMPILE FIXTURE. A caller authors a MANIFEST of `.dag` modules and
// an entry among them; the instrument compiles exactly those bytes through the v1 pipeline with
// no module index, no corpus roots, and no filesystem read, and returns what the compile decided.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS BESIDE THE SINGLE-MODULE CENSUS RATHER THAN INSIDE IT. `compile_dag_diagnostic_census`
// resolves its one synthetic module's imports against the live checkout, so the corpus is always
// in the pool and a SECOND module can never be authored. That makes every question whose subject
// is the relationship between two modules unaskable on it: a cross-module name collision, an
// import that must not bind, a spelling held by both a fixture declaration and a corpus one.
// Three consumers hit that wall independently before this instrument existed -- invalid fixtures
// relocated out of the corpus for want of it, an ambiguity arm measured and deleted for want of
// it, and DESIGN naming the missing multi-module compile fixture as the next-rung trigger for the
// acyclicity class, whose own receipt records that the single-module harness returns ParseError
// for the ACYCLIC control as well as the cyclic one and so could never reach the wall.
//
// IT IS AN INSTRUMENT AND NOT A GATE. Its outcome reports what the compile decided; nothing here
// judges acceptance, and no workflow invokes it. Callers judge.

// CORPUS ISOLATION IS BY CONSTRUCTION, NOT BY A FLAG, and that is the property the whole instrument
// rests on. The supplied manifest IS the source vector handed to the pipeline: there is no code
// path in the host realization that consults a module index, so a fixture module may reuse a corpus
// module's SPELLING and bind its own declaration. THE OPEN QUESTION THIS ANSWERS, recorded because
// upstream did not know the answer and asked for it either way: under whole-corpus floor
// preparation the required floor folds every witness through ONE prepared subject, so a name
// collision between a witness and the corpus is unconditional there. That preparation is a fact
// about how WITNESSES are compiled, not about this nested compile - the prepared authority is
// consulted by unrelated censuses and never by compile_to_resolved - so a fixture compiled through
// this instrument is isolated even when the caller itself was prepared whole-corpus. Control 5 of
// the witness measures exactly that rather than assuming it.

// PHASE-GRAINED STANDING IS THE POINT OF THE COPRODUCT. FixtureInstrumentRefused is the HARNESS's
// fault - a ragged manifest, a duplicate path, an entry naming no supplied module, a panic.
// FixtureCompileRefused is the SUBJECT's fault and carries the compiler's own judgment.
// FixtureCompileCompleted is the subject passing. Collapsing the first two hands a broken harness
// the compiler's message, which is the not-applicable-versus-malformed conflation DESIGN records as
// a recurring failure mode, applied to the instrument itself: one reason symbol over 'the input is
// wrong' and 'this instrument had nothing to say about it', which have opposite owners and opposite
// repairs. EXECUTION PROVENANCE IS STRUCTURAL AND NOT A FIELD ANYONE MUST REMEMBER TO READ:
// FixtureCompileCompleted is constructed at exactly one place in the host, after the emit returned
// and after the blocking population was counted, so an unreached or killed compile HAS NO SPELLING
// in which it renders as zero diagnostics. That is the positive-artifact rule moved into the type
// rather than asserted about it.

// THE TWO DIGESTS TRAVEL WITH THE RESULT so a receipt quoting this instrument names the subject it
// measured and the compiler that measured it. source_digest is a structural fnv1a64 over the entry
// spelling and every (path, content) pair in the order supplied - a reordering is a different
// subject and a whitespace edit is a different subject. compiler_digest is resolved_graph_cache
// transform_content_digest, the content hash of the running compiler binary, which is already the
// single authority every identity key in the seed consumes for compiler identity rather than a
// second executable read. Both are absent from the FixtureInstrumentRefused arm on purpose: that
// arm reports that no subject was formed, and stamping a digest on it would name a measurement that
// did not happen.

// SCOPE, stated so a receipt cannot claim coverage it does not have (DESIGN 4b: a class's rung is
// the MINIMUM across its in-scope paths). This is the v1 pipeline to the RUST render target over an
// authored multi-module subject. It observes NOTHING about the interpreter's disposition of the
// same program, nothing about other emission targets, and nothing about corpus-grain prevalence.
// Unlike the single-module census it does NOT arm with_type_ref_hit_ne_bind_measure: the census
// arms that bracket to sharpen masked type refs against a corpus pool this instrument does not
// have, so arming it here would be a knob with no subject. WHAT module_count MEANS: the number of
// modules the RESOLVE stage produced, read from the resolved graph - not the length of the supplied
// list. A control comparing it against the manifest is therefore a join between two different
// producers rather than measure() == measure().

type FixtureSource {
  path: NonEmptyStr
  content: String
}

type MultiModuleCompileFixture {
  sources: List<FixtureSource>
  entry: NonEmptyStr
}

// WHAT CALLABLE LOOKUP DECIDED, REPORTED RATHER THAN RECOMPUTED, AND WHY IT IS HERE RATHER THAN IN
// A SECOND BUILTIN. The question "which declaration did this call actually bind" has no observer
// over supplied sources: the diagnostic census answers accept/refuse, and the reference-occurrence
// census answers with a provider drawn from the STRUCTURAL CANDIDATE walk, which is a different
// traversal from the callable lookup that constructs CallTargetIdentity. Answering with the wrong
// walk is the substitution this repository keeps finding, so neither existing surface can serve.
//
// A second supplied-source builtin would have been a SECOND NAME for one concept -- compile these
// authored modules and report something about the result -- which is the nicknaming DESIGN section 3
// forbids, and it would double the surface the seed has to delete as it shrinks. One builtin, a
// richer observation.
//
// THE ROWS RESOLVE NOTHING. ExprCall carries call_semantics, which v1.compiler.infer lookup WRITES
// when it resolves a call; the host projection reads that field over the already-resolved graph. No
// lookup is invoked and no environment is constructed, so this observes the path the corpus takes
// rather than a path only the instrument takes.
//
// KEYED BY THE AUTHORED CALL OCCURRENCE, never summarised per module: a module-grain list cannot say
// WHICH call node got WHICH target, and the subject this exists for is one bare call against its
// qualified twin inside one module.
//
// CallSemanticsAbsent IS NOT CallTargetUndetermined. The second is lookup's own verdict that no
// target was determinable; the first says lookup never wrote here at all. Collapsing them would let
// a control read a silent pass as a decided refusal, which is the could-not-measure conflation
// DESIGN section 5 forbids.
// ADMITTED AGAINST THE v1 FREEZE BY PURPOSE, AND NOT BY RESEMBLANCE TO ANOTHER ADMITTED ROW.
// gunbc.v1_maintenance_standing v1_seed_standing admits a change when it serves the v2 self-host
// program and keeps v1 closed to growth for its own sake -- and it names the standing danger that an
// active-maintenance arm becomes an ABSORBING one if every proposal classifies as admissible. So
// "the reference-occurrence census builtin was allowed" is deliberately NOT the argument here: that
// is a precedent, and precedent is exactly how the absorbing arm forms.
//
// WHAT IT SERVES. The import-deletion program's Step 1 gate is that an import declaration has ZERO
// BINDING AUTHORITY: paired subjects differing only in which provider is imported, or in whether one
// is imported at all, must produce the SAME resolver outcome for a bare call. That gate is not
// assertable without observing WHICH DECLARATION a call bound, at owner-and-name grain.
//
// WHY EVERY ALTERNATIVE FAILS, checked rather than asserted. Accept/refuse cannot express it: two
// providers declaring one spelling with IDENTICAL SIGNATURES both typecheck, so a wrong selection is
// compile-green and no diagnostic exists to match on. The reference-occurrence census reports a
// provider drawn from the STRUCTURAL CANDIDATE walk, which is a different traversal from the callable
// lookup that constructs CallTargetIdentity -- answering with it would look like an answer and be one
// to a different question, which is the substitution this repository keeps finding. The
// declared-import-closure observer carries a definer module but observes the CLOSURE path, and the
// survivor the Step 1 root correction exists to catch is authored_import_names consumed INSIDE
// callable lookup, which that observer never consults; it would produce a green that means nothing.
// Reading the emitted Rust infers the target from behaviour, which is the fabricated-plausible-output
// direction DESIGN section 5 forbids and which no gate should rest on.
//
// CLASSIFIED AGAINST THE FIVE REFUSED CLASSES. NewLanguageBehavior: NO -- the compile pipeline is
// byte-untouched; nothing is added, moved or removed in resolve, infer or emit, and this reads a
// field lookup already wrote. NewCompatibilityObligation: NO -- no caller is kept working; the
// outcome coproduct gains a field and every existing consumer matches the coproduct rather than
// destructuring positionally. NewEscapeHatchOrAdmissionRow: NO, and the direction is opposite --
// this adds an observation that makes a wall assertable and removes nothing; there is no flag, mode
// or env var, and no write path. SeedFeatureCompletion: NO -- the seed COMPILER gains no capability
// and compiles exactly what it compiled before. PublicSurfaceGrowth: this is the nearest and it is
// the honest cost -- one coproduct, one record and one field on an existing result, on an instrument
// that already existed. No new builtin, no new entry point, no second supplied-source surface.
//
// DISSOLUTION, NAMED AS A CAPABILITY AND NOT AS AN ARTIFACT. This projection retires when v2's own
// callable lookup is the authority that answers "which declaration did this call bind" for an
// authored manifest -- that is, when the resolution that decides a corpus call and the resolution a
// fixture observes are ONE implementation rather than a seed one and a substrate one. Retiring it on
// anything less -- a v2 module that merely exists, a fixture harness that merely runs -- would leave
// the question answered by the seed while the row claimed the substrate answered it.

type ResolvedCallTarget
  = CallBoundToSourceDeclaration { owner_module_path: String, decl_name: String }
  | CallBoundToRuntimePrimitive { primitive_name: String }
  | CallTargetUndetermined
  | CallSemanticsAbsent

type ResolvedCallTargetRow {
  occurrence: Int
  has_occurrence: Bool
  call_file: String
  call_module: String
  authored_name: String
  span_start: Int
  target: ResolvedCallTarget
}

type MultiModuleCompileFixtureOutcome
  = FixtureInstrumentRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }
  | FixtureCompileRefused {
      module_count: Int
      diagnostics: List<CompileDiagnosticCensusRow>
      call_targets: List<ResolvedCallTargetRow>
      source_digest: NonEmptyStr
      compiler_digest: NonEmptyStr
    }
  | FixtureCompileCompleted {
      module_count: Int
      emitted_files: List<String>
      diagnostics: List<CompileDiagnosticCensusRow>
      call_targets: List<ResolvedCallTargetRow>
      source_digest: NonEmptyStr
      compiler_digest: NonEmptyStr
    }

fn fixture_source(path: String, content: String) -> FixtureSource {
  FixtureSource { path: path, content: content }
}

fn compile_fixture(fixture: MultiModuleCompileFixture) -> MultiModuleCompileFixtureOutcome {
  compile_dag_multi_module_fixture(
    fixture.sources |> map(s => s.path as String),
    fixture.sources |> map(s => s.content),
    fixture.entry as String
  )
}

// PURE READS OVER ROWS, never over the outcome -- the same construction gunbc.compile_diagnostic_census
// adopted after an accessor answering [] on the not-runnable arm was found to be the fail-open it
// exists to prevent. The only way to obtain a diagnostic list here is to MATCH the coproduct, so
// reading zero diagnostics out of a refused instrument is structurally unwritable.
fn fixture_blocking_rows(rows: List<CompileDiagnosticCensusRow>) -> List<CompileDiagnosticCensusRow> {
  rows |> filter(r => r.blocking)
}

fn fixture_rows_of_class(rows: List<CompileDiagnosticCensusRow>, wanted: String) -> List<CompileDiagnosticCensusRow> {
  rows |> filter(r => (r.diagnostic_class as String) == wanted)
}

fn fixture_has_class(rows: List<CompileDiagnosticCensusRow>, wanted: String) -> Bool {
  count(fixture_rows_of_class(rows, wanted)) > 0
}
